{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "תועה", :translit "to'eh", :gloss "wandering"} {:he "חמורו תועה", :translit "chamoro to'eh", :gloss "his ass wandering"} {:he "אדם תועה מדרך השכל", :translit "adam to'eh mi-derech ha-sechel", :gloss "a man wandering from the path of reason"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["תועה" "חמורו תועה" "אדם תועה מדרך השכל"]}], :frame {:text "The three occurrences of תועה build a moral reading: Joseph's brothers failed at the obligation encoded in the phrase אדם תועה מדרך השכל. The Ba'al HaTurim's language about returning a wanderer echoes the talmudic obligation to return a lost person, derived from the lost-property commandments in Deuteronomy 22:1–3 as developed in Bava Metzia 30b and expanded in Shulchan Arukh, Choshen Mishpat 259.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 22:1-3" "Bava Metzia 30b" "Shulchan Arukh Choshen Mishpat 259"]}} {:quotes [{:he "וישאלהו האיש", :translit "va-yish'alayhu ha-ish", :gloss "and the man asked him"} {:he "מלאך גבריאל", :translit "malakh gavriel", :gloss "the angel Gabriel"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "וישאלהו האיש", :b "מלאך גבריאל"}], :frame {:text "The unidentified 'man' (איש) who directs Joseph toward his brothers is identified in rabbinic tradition as the angel Gabriel. This identification appears in Tanchuma, Vayeishev 2 and is echoed in Rashi on Genesis 37:15 citing the same midrashic source.", :sources ["Tanchuma Vayeishev 2" "Rashi on Genesis 37:15"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["תועה" "חמורו תועה" "אדם תועה מדרך השכל"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "תועה", :refs ["Isaiah 32:6" "Proverbs 21:16" "Nehemiah 4:2"], :n 3, :lens :exact} {:phrase "חמורו תועה", :refs ["Exodus 23:4"], :n 1, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "אדם תועה מדרך השכל", :refs ["Proverbs 21:16"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :gematria, :a "וישאלהו האיש", :a-value 674, :b "מלאך גבריאל", :b-value 337, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false}], :book :genesis, :voi